  "slug": "automated-microscopy-2e31",
  "summary": "Recent preprints address ambient RNA, doublets, batch effects and segmentation to attribute single-cell and spatial measurements to underlying sources.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread in these preprints is separating biological structure from technical contributions before assigning measurements to cells, states or spatial domains. PRISM jointly models ambient RNA, doublets and cell-type assignment in an EM framework and is benchmarked in silico. stochprofIC applies distributional regression to correct noise and batch effects when inferring single-cell state variability from mini-bulk and split-pool data. spSheaf bypasses cell segmentation altogether, using cellular sheaf theory on raw transcript molecules to infer spatial domains and outperforming SSAM and Points2Regions. Cross-modal anchoring corrects batch effects in one assay using a stable paired omic layer without covariates, outperforming ComBat on TCGA. KQE-2 addresses reproducibility of single-cell clustering across 11.4 million nuclei with a training-free hybrid quantum-classical approach, a more distinct direction from the rest of the set.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-15"
}
